--- [number_theory.floor_function.modular_floor] ---
Triggers: floor function, modular arithmetic, Diophantine with floor
Technique: Since x/7 must be an integer (as floor outputs are integers), write x=7k. Then floor(7k/2)-floor(7k/3)=k. Use fractional part analysis: {7k/2} and {7k/3} depend on k mod 6. For each residue class k mod 6, the equation becomes linear in k with explicit constants from the fractional parts. Solve each case and sum all valid real solutions.
Question: Compute the sum of all real numbers $x$ such that

$$
\left\lfloor\frac{x}{2}\right\rfloor-\left\lfloor\frac{x}{3}\right\rfloor=\frac{x}{7}
$$
Answer: The RHS x/7 is the difference of two integers, so x is a multiple of 7. Write x=7k. Then floor(7k/2)-floor(7k/3)=k becomes (7k/2 - {7k/2}) - (7k/3 - {7k/3}) = k, simplifying to 7k/6 + {7k/3} - {7k/2} = k, i.e., k/6 = {7k/2} - {7k/3}. Since fractional parts are periodic mod lcm(2,3)=6, check k mod 6 in each residue class. Each case gives a specific value, yielding k/6 = constant. Solve for k in each case, recover x=7k, and sum.

