        \[
\text{Maximum number of faces }m=52 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 1.  Combinatorial relations  

Let  

* \(F=m\) – number of triangular faces,  
* \(E\) – number of edges,  
* \(V\) – number of vertices,  

and denote by  

\[
N_{a,b}\;(a,b\ge 0)
\]

the number of vertices at which exactly  

* \(a\) faces meet with their \(30^{\circ}\)‑angle, and  
* \(b\) faces meet with their \(75^{\circ}\)‑angle  

(\(a+b\) is the degree of the vertex).  
Because each face is an isosceles triangle with one \(30^{\circ}\)‑angle and two
\(75^{\circ}\)‑angles we have  

\[
\sum_{a,b} a\,N_{a,b}=m ,\qquad 
\sum_{a,b} b\,N_{a,b}=2m .\tag{1}
\]

Every edge belongs to two faces, therefore  

\[
3F=2E\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad E=\frac{3m}{2}. \tag{2}
\]

At a vertex the sum of the incident face–angles is \(30a+75b\);
convexity forces  

\[
30a+75b<360^{\circ}. \tag{3}
\]

The angular deficit at a vertex equals  

\[
\delta_{a,b}=360^{\circ}-(30a+75b) .
\]

For a convex polyhedron the total deficit is \(720^{\circ}\) (Gauss–Bonnet), whence  

\[
\sum_{a,b}\delta_{a,b}N_{a,b}=720^{\circ}. \tag{4}
\]

Using (1) in (4) we obtain  

\[
720^{\circ}=360^{\circ}V-30m-75(2m)
            =360^{\circ}V-180^{\circ}m,
\]
so that  

\[
V=\frac{m+4}{2}. \tag{5}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 2.  A bound for \(m\)

From (3) the possible vertex‑types \((a,b)\) are

\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
(a,b) & \delta_{a,b}\ (\text{degrees})\\ \hline
(0,3) &135\\
(0,4) & 60\\
(1,2) &180\\
(1,3) &105\\
(1,4) & 30\\
(2,1) &225\\
(2,2) &150\\
(2,3) & 75\\
(3,0) &270\\
(3,1) &195\\
(3,2) &120\\
(3,3) & 45\\
(4,0) &240\\
(4,1) &165\\
(4,2) & 90\\
\mathbf{(4,3)} &\mathbf{15}\\
\vdots &\vdots
\end{array}
\]

The smallest possible deficit is \(15^{\circ}\), attained only at the
type \((4,3)\).  Consequently

\[
720^{\circ}= \sum \delta_{a,b}N_{a,b}\ge 15^{\circ}\sum N_{a,b}=15^{\circ}V .
\]

Using (5),

\[
15\,V\le 720\quad\Longrightarrow\quad V\le 48\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\frac{m+4}{2}\le 48,
\]
hence  

\[
m\le 52 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 3.  Realising \(m=52\)

For \(m=52\) we have \(V=28\) and the total deficit is exactly
\(48\cdot15^{\circ}\).  A solution of the linear system (1)–(5) is

\[
\begin{aligned}
&N_{1,4}=20 &&\text{(type }(1,4),\; \delta=30^{\circ}),\\
&N_{4,3}= 8 &&\text{(type }(4,3),\; \delta=15^{\circ}),
\end{aligned}
\]
all other \(N_{a,b}=0\).
Indeed  

\[
\begin{aligned}
\sum N_{a,b}&=20+8=28=V,\\
\sum aN_{a,b}&=20\cdot1+8\cdot4=52=m,\\
\sum bN_{a,b}&=20\cdot4+8\cdot3=104=2m,\\
\sum\delta_{a,b}N_{a,b}&=20\cdot30^{\circ}+8\cdot15^{\circ}=720^{\circ}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Thus a planar triangulation of the sphere exists with  

* 20 vertices of degree \(5\) (type \((1,4)\)), and  
* 8 vertices of degree \(7\) (type \((4,3)\)).

Give each triangle a distinguished vertex – the one where the
\(30^{\circ}\) angle occurs.  At a vertex of type \((1,4)\) exactly one
incident triangle uses that vertex as its distinguished corner; at a
vertex of type \((4,3)\) exactly four incident triangles do so.
Consequently every triangle has precisely one distinguished corner,
so the three angles of each face are \(30^{\circ},75^{\circ},75^{\circ}\).

By Steinitz’s theorem every 3‑connected planar triangulation is the
edge‑graph of a convex polyhedron.  Realising the above triangulation as a
convex polyhedron and interpreting the distinguished corners as the
\(30^{\circ}\) angles yields a convex polyhedron whose faces are exactly
\(52\) congruent triangles with angles \(30^{\circ},75^{\circ},75^{\circ}\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 4.  Conclusion  

The angular‑deficit argument gives the universal bound \(m\le 52\);
the explicit triangulation described above shows that \(m=52\) is
attainable.  Hence

\[
\boxed{\,\displaystyle \max m = 52\, } .
\]

