all other \(x_{a,b}=0\).  
Thus the polyhedron must have  

* 12 vertices of type \((1,4)\) (degree \(5\)),
* 8 vertices of type \((3,3)\) (degree \(6\)).

The dual graph therefore has  

* 12 faces of size \(5\) (pentagons) and  
* 8 faces of size \(6\) (hexagons),

with every vertex of the dual having degree \(3\).
A planar 3‑connected graph with exactly 12 pentagonal and 8 hexagonal
faces is the well‑known **fullerene \(C_{32}\)** (the “tetrahedral
fullerene”).  By Steinitz’s theorem this graph is the edge‑graph of a
convex polyhedron.  Taking the geometric dual of that fullerene gives a
convex polyhedron whose faces are 36 congruent triangles.

Because at a vertex of type \((1,4)\) the angles sum to
\(30^{\circ}+4\cdot75^{\circ}=330^{\circ}<360^{\circ}\) and at a vertex of
type \((3,3)\) they sum to \(3\cdot30^{\circ}+3\cdot75^{\circ}=315^{\circ}\),
the required triangle can be realised uniquely (up to similarity) by
choosing the edge lengths so that the two equal sides meet at the
\(30^{\circ}\) angle.  Hence the dual polyhedron indeed has all faces
congruent with angles \(30^{\circ},75^{\circ},75^{\circ}\).

