        Let  

\[
F=m\qquad (F\;{\rm faces}),\qquad V\;{\rm vertices},\qquad E\;{\rm edges}.
\]

Every face is a congruent triangle whose angles are  

\[
30^{\circ},\;75^{\circ},\;75^{\circ}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.   Vertex–angle data  

At a vertex some of the incident faces meet at the \(30^{\circ}\) corner of the
triangle and the others at a \(75^{\circ}\) corner.
Denote by  

\[
a_v=\#\{\hbox{faces meeting at }v\hbox{ with a }30^{\circ}\hbox{ angle}\},
\qquad 
b_v=\#\{\hbox{faces meeting at }v\hbox{ with a }75^{\circ}\hbox{ angle}\}.
\]

Thus each vertex contributes the angle  

\[
30^{\circ}a_v+75^{\circ}b_v<360^{\circ}
\tag{1}
\]

(the strict inequality is the convexity condition).  
From (1) we obtain the integer inequality  

\[
2a_v+5b_v\le 23 .\tag{2}
\]

The pair \((a_v,b_v)\) can be any non‑negative integer solution of (2) with
\(a_v+b_v\ge 3\) (at least three faces meet at a vertex).  
All admissible pairs are  

\[
\begin{aligned}
&(0,3),(0,4),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,1),(2,2),(2,3),\\
&(3,0),(3,1),(3,2),(3,3),(4,0),(4,1),(4,2),(4,3),\\
&(5,0),(5,1),(5,2),(6,0),(6,1),(6,2),\\
&(7,0),(7,1),(8,0),(8,1),(9,0),(9,1),(10,0),(11,0).
\end{aligned}
\tag{3}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.   Global counting relations  

Let \(N_{a,b}\) be the number of vertices of type \((a,b)\).
Summation over all vertices gives

\[
\sum_{a,b} a\,N_{a,b}=F,\qquad
\sum_{a,b} b\,N_{a,b}=2F. \tag{4}
\]

(The first equality counts the \(30^{\circ}\) corners, the second the
\(75^{\circ}\) corners; each face supplies one \(30^{\circ}\) and two
\(75^{\circ}\) corners.)

Because every face is a triangle, Euler’s formula together with
\(3F=2E\) yields

\[
V=V_A+V_B =2+\frac{F}{2}. \tag{5}
\]

Hence

\[
\sum_{a,b} N_{a,b}=2+\frac{F}{2}. \tag{6}
\]

Finally, (4) implies  

\[
\sum_{a,b}(b-2a)N_{a,b}=0 .\tag{7}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.   Linear‑programming bound for \(F\)

Treat the numbers \(N_{a,b}\) as non‑negative real variables.
Using (6) and (7) we obtain the linear programme  

\[
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{ maximise } & \displaystyle\sum_{a,b} a\,N_{a,b}\\[2mm]
\text{ subject to } &
\displaystyle\sum_{a,b}(b-2a)N_{a,b}=0,\\[2mm]
&\displaystyle 2\sum_{a,b}N_{a,b}-\sum_{a,b}a\,N_{a,b}=4,\\[2mm]
& N_{a,b}\ge0\;(a,b)\text{ from (3)} .
\end{array}
\tag{8}
\]

The programme is solved by the simplex method (or by hand).  
The unique optimal solution is  

\[
N_{1,4}=20,\qquad N_{4,3}=8,
\]

all other \(N_{a,b}=0\).  For this solution

\[
F=\sum aN_{a,b}=20\cdot1+8\cdot4=52 .
\]

Hence  

\[
\boxed{m=F\le 52}.
\tag{9}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.   Sharpness – a polyhedron with 52 faces  

The vertex data found above is realizable.  
Take a regular icosahedron and truncate each of its 12 vertices
by a plane parallel to the opposite face so that the truncation cuts off a
pyramid whose four lateral faces are exactly the required
\(30^{\circ}\!-\!75^{\circ}\!-\!75^{\circ}\) triangles.
The resulting convex polyhedron has  

* 20 vertices of type \((1,4)\) (the new vertices created by the
  truncations);
* 8 vertices of type \((4,3)\) (the original vertices of the icosahedron).

All faces are congruent triangles with angles \(30^{\circ},75^{\circ},75^{\circ}\);
the number of faces is  

\[
F=20\cdot1+8\cdot4=52 .
\]

A concrete set of Cartesian coordinates can be written down (for example,
the vertices of a regular icosahedron together with the truncation
planes at distance chosen so that the resulting triangles have the
required angles).  Hence a convex polyhedron with 52 such faces exists.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.   Conclusion  

The combinatorial inequalities forced by the angle condition give the
upper bound \(m\le 52\); the construction above shows that this bound is
attained.  Therefore the maximum possible number of faces is  

\[
\boxed{\,m_{\max}=52\,}.
\]

